  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Good envelope-layer substrate for carrying multiple peer advertisements without central coordination, but it does not itself define chunk-ad semantics, duplicate handling, or Bob's local peer-choice accounting, so scenario coverage is only partial.",
    "strengths": [
      "Explicit pCID dispatch and no-speculative-parse rule give very clear layer boundaries.",
      "Unknown-pCID store-and-forward plus canonical DAG-CBOR bytes support sparse knowledge and protocol evolution.",
      "Wrapper-based signature/evidence protocols can support peer-specific offers without requiring a central reputation service."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The spec does not define the chunk advertisement payload or duplicate-offer semantics.",
      "Bob's peer-selection and promise-accounting logic are left entirely to companion protocols or local policy.",
      "No fixed signature slot means cross-peer comparison may depend on extra wrapper conventions."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Semantically identical offers wrapped differently may be harder to deduplicate and audit consistently.",
      "Peers without the relevant pCID handlers can preserve advertisements but cannot evaluate them.",
      "Because the specimen is still a draft, long-term interoperability depends on later freezing companion protocols and pCID conventions."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What payload protocol identifies chunk C and makes duplicate advertisements harmless rather than ambiguous?",
      "How are Alice and Carol's identities and delivery promises bound to the offer when signatures live in wrapper protocols?",
      "How does Bob keep durable local accounting records and recover needed pCID/spec material over decades without creating a central registry?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
